fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1069", description: some(value: "A `provides` CLAUSE'S BINDINGS ARE NOT A CARRIER SELECTOR, AND NOTHING SAYS SO — a\nclause naming another carrier files SILENTLY under the sort it is written in.\n\nMEASURED (WI-1000), both rows on one program:\n\n  namespace Rec  provides Show[T = Other]  end   ->  SortProvidesInfo(Rec,   Show)\n  fact Show[T = Other]        one level out      ->  SortProvidesInfo(Other, Show)\n\n`load_provides_clause` (kb/load.rs) takes the PROVIDING SORT from `domain` — the\nenclosing scope's owner — and the clause's bindings are the SPEC's arguments. So\n`provides Show[T = Other]` says \"the enclosing sort provides Show at T = Other\",\nwhich is almost never what someone writing `T = Other` means. It does not refuse,\nit does not warn: the program loads and the wrong carrier is on record, which is\nthe input to the backing obligation, coherence and dispatch alike.\n\nNOT INTRODUCED BY WI-1000. The shape has always been admissible inside a `sort X …\nend` body, where it behaves identically. WI-1000 admitted `provides_clause` in a\nnamespace body (059 R3 needs it: a secondary entry's allowed spelling of a spec\nclaim), so the same trap is now reachable one more place — and it refuses the\ngenuinely subjectless case, a `provides` in a namespace with no sort at its address\n(`LoadError::ProvidesClauseNeedsSort`). What it did NOT do is decide this one.\n\nWHAT MUST BE MEASURED BEFORE ANY REFUSAL, and it is the whole cost of the change:\nHOW MANY `provides` CLAUSES IN stdlib / anthill-stl / anthill-cpp-gen / examples /\nanthill-todo BIND THE SPEC'S CARRIER PARAMETER TO SOMETHING THAT IS NOT THE\nENCLOSING SORT. The count decides whether this lands as a refusal, a warning, or\nnothing. A legitimate population plausibly exists — a spec parameter that is not\nthe carrier (`provides Effect[T = ConsoleOutput]` on `sort Console`, kernel-language\n§8.7 \"one carrier declaring one spec twice\") binds a non-self type ON PURPOSE, so\nthe rule cannot be \"every binding must name the enclosing sort\". The discriminator\nhas to be the SPEC'S CARRIER PARAMETER specifically, and identifying that parameter\nis itself unsettled — nothing declares a sort to BE a spec (059's fact-ban section:\nno `spec` keyword, no `SymbolKind::Spec`, every reader infers it from shape).\n\nSO THIS IS NOT A ONE-LINE GUARD, which is why it is a ticket and not an inline fix.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the corpus count above is MEASURED and reported first, naming each site;\nthen either (a) a refusal naming the clause, the binding and the enclosing sort, with\na control showing the legitimate non-carrier-parameter population still loads, or (b)\na recorded decision that the shape is legal with the spec sentence to match. Either\nway kernel-language.md §5.1's paragraph \"A `provides` clause names its subject by\nWHERE it is written\" is updated to say what the bindings mean. Each test says which\ncase fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1000 (measured it, admitted the production in a namespace body,\n`ProvidesClauseNeedsSort`), 059 R3 \"A SPEC CLAIM, written `provides`\", 058 §4\n(retiring the `fact X[…]` spelling — this is the question that spelling change\nwould inherit), WI-1032 (two provisions of one spec at one application), §8.7."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-11T12:59:40Z"))
